fix(ui): F-0029 Turning automatic dosing back on asks for confirmation first

Finding: F-0029 (P1, H5 error prevention; H3).
Baseline: apply after F-0025.patch.

Contract
  Originating: from manual, a single tap on the switch no longer turns automatic dosing on; an explicit
  "Turn automatic on" is required. Switching to manual stays a single tap (the critical journey).
  Regression: journey switch-dosing-to-manual; console, axe, keyboard, targets, states, live-regions,
  contrast, layout, census, copy, motion; no introduced finding.
  Scope: index.html dosing section; dashboard.js; styles.css .confirm/.button.
Drift class and layer: local defect; behaviour, with the smallest markup and CSS it needs. An inline
confirmation (not a modal) sits below the switch. Focus moves to "Turn automatic on"; "Keep manual" or Escape
closes it and returns focus to the switch. The colours come from the existing --ink and --page tokens.
Assumptions (inferred, please confirm):
  - Asking only in this direction is enough. Switching to manual stops the pumps and is the safe direction.
  - The wording "Check the reservoir is full first: pumps A and B will start topping up to EC 1.8." restates
    the automatic status line already on the page, plus the journey's own fact that the pumps must not
    dose into an empty tank.
Left alone: the reload behaviour (F-0022, waiting on the owner) and the pump-start control mentioned in the
manual message (F-0030, P2).
No test runner exists in the project, so the behaviour is guarded by probes instead of a unit test.
Re-test: run 20261006-155844-fix-f-0029.
  Probe 1 (768 px, pointer), 16/16 steps: one tap goes to manual; a second tap keeps manual and shows the
  confirmation; "Keep manual" keeps manual; Escape closes it; "Turn automatic on" sets automatic.
  Probe 2 (375 px, keyboard), 8/8 steps: Enter goes to manual; Space opens the confirmation; Escape closes it and
  dosing stays manual.
  Console: 0 errors. The journey passed (3/3). No finding was introduced. The introduced instances listed in this
  run's diff (F-0007, F-0013) belong to the F-0025 alarm chip, not to this change.

